From 3fbad94f87568af720202bf25d596471111c7145 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 10:12:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Astrology: a night ground, scoped to the hub and guarded
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Answers the question the Relief rollout left open — whether the tarot page
and the letter should keep a night surface as a deliberate one-room
exception, "a decision to make before this lands, not after". It landed
white and the decision was never made. This is that decision, made the way
the press exception was: named in tokens.css, scoped to one hub, pinned by
a guard.

Three things say the room was always meant to be dark: the gold-on-black
artwork it was drawn for is still in the tree, the hub config still declares
dark: true (read by nothing since the rollout), and the near-white guard
records the casualty of turning it white - cream text on a surface that had
stopped being dark, on the page whose heading says "Birth Details".

Not a dark mode. A dark mode is a second copy of every screen behind a
switch, and half-converted it reads as a bug. This is one room with its own
light: nothing toggles, there is no [data-theme], and a citizen who never
opens Astrology never sees a dark pixel.

The relief is unchanged - five depths, no sixth, same geometry. On white the
highlight was invisible and the shadow did the work; on charcoal the shadow
has nothing to darken and the highlight does it, so the rim's bright edge
stays on top at a much lower alpha and the drop shadow deepens.

THE CITY'S FURNITURE STAYS THE CITY'S. The orange lamp, --on-accent and the
black primary button are deliberately NOT re-pointed. --on-accent is read by
seven surfaces and every one of them is dark; giving the hub a gold FILL
would have required inverting it, which turns the black button's own label
black. The metal lives in --accent-ink, where it is text, and --accent stays
dark enough for the white the city puts on it.

The rail WELL does follow the room, because it must: every label on it reads
--ink and --faint, so a white well under ivory text is the hub name gone.

One latent bug, fixed in one word: .btn-secondary was `background: #fff`
under `color: var(--ink)` — a face that follows nothing beneath a label that
follows the room. It is var(--card) now, which IS #ffffff at the root, so
nothing changes for the other twenty-four hubs. It was the only hardcoded
light background in the material; a guard now keeps it the only one.

Guards: the ground exception names two hubs and still refuses a third; the
night hub must re-point its own ink and its well, must NOT re-point the
city's furniture, and may never acquire a theme switch. All verified by
breaking them.

